{{- $mail := fromToml (includeTemplate "mail-identities.toml" .) -}}
{{- $primary := index $mail "primary" -}}
{{- if $primary -}}
# mbsync (isync) — IMAP <-> Maildir sync for the HEY-style Emacs mail setup.
# packages/manifest.toml: isync. Read by `mbsync -a`, by bin/mail-sync and by
# imapnotify-icloud.service on every IDLE event.
#
# A TEMPLATE rather than a plain symlinked file on purpose. chezmoi is in
# symlink mode here, so a non-templated entry would leave ~/.mbsyncrc pointing
# into ~/.dotfiles — a PUBLIC repo (github.com/saifulapm/dotfiles), where git
# tracks only the executable bit and a fresh clone lands 0644. Templates are
# WRITTEN instead of linked, so `private_` actually produces 0600 on disk.
# There is no password in this file either way (PassCmd shells out to `pass`)
# and no address either — the account comes from the age-encrypted
# .chezmoitemplates/mail-identities.toml.age, so the public repo never names
# it. The whole file sits inside that data's guard: no age key on the machine,
# no ~/.mbsyncrc at all, which is what keeps a phase-1 install from rendering
# a half-configured one (see .chezmoitemplates/mail-identities.toml).
#
# FORMAT WARNING, learned the hard way: a BLANK LINE ENDS A SECTION in
# mbsyncrc. Comment lines are fine inside one, empty lines are not — an
# accidental blank between `IMAPAccount` and `PassCmd` makes mbsync report
# "'PassCmd' is not a recognized section-starting or global keyword", which
# reads like the keyword is unsupported rather than misplaced. Every section
# below is therefore one contiguous run of lines, with the prose above it.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- iCloud
# Host/port: implicit TLS on 993; iCloud offers no STARTTLS on 143 at all.
# PassCmd: the app-specific password from appleid.apple.com, stored in `pass`
#   as a multi-line entry (line 1 = the secret, the rest is host/port and
#   revocation notes), hence `head -n1`. Apple rejects the ordinary Apple ID
#   password on IMAP outright, so a login failure here is nearly always a
#   revoked or regenerated app-specific password — make a new one, `pass edit`
#   it in, nothing else changes.
# PipelineDepth/Timeout: iCloud is the flaky half of this setup — it drops
#   IMAP connections mid-fetch under load, which is why the macOS config
#   capped each sync at 120s and told you to just poll again. Shallow
#   pipelining trades a little throughput for far fewer mid-fetch resets.
IMAPAccount icloud
Host imap.mail.me.com
Port 993
User {{ $primary }}
PassCmd "pass email/icloud/{{ (splitList "@" $primary) | first }} | head -n1"
TLSType IMAPS
SystemCertificates yes
PipelineDepth 10
Timeout 120

IMAPStore icloud-remote
Account icloud

# SubFolders Verbatim keeps iCloud's own names ("Sent Messages", "Deleted
# Messages") as real directories instead of Maildir++ dot-folders. notmuch
# indexes either shape, but verbatim is what makes `notmuch search
# folder:"Sent Messages"` read like the mailbox actually looks — and it is what
# the tag->folder step (phone parity, bin/hey-folder-sync) writes into: that
# script creates ~/Mail/icloud/HEY/<Box>/{cur,new,tmp} and Create Both below
# turns it into a real "HEY/Box" folder on iCloud. Verbatim is what makes the
# on-disk path and the IMAP name the same string, so `folder:"icloud/HEY/Feed"`
# in notmuch and HEY/Feed on the iPhone are provably the same folder.
MaildirStore icloud-local
Path ~/Mail/icloud/
Inbox ~/Mail/icloud/INBOX
SubFolders Verbatim

# Create Both: folders made on either side appear on the other. This is now
#   load-bearing, not a precaution: bin/hey-folder-sync creates HEY/<Box>
#   locally the first time a box has anything to file, and this line is the
#   only thing that turns that into a folder the iPhone can see. iCloud creates
#   the "HEY" parent implicitly from "HEY/Screener" — verified against the
#   account, which uses "/" as its hierarchy delimiter.
# Remove None: deliberately NOT `Remove Both`. A local indexing accident must
#   never be able to delete a mailbox off the server. Folder removals stay a
#   manual, deliberate act.
# `Patterns *` covers the HEY folders with no edit needed — an IMAP4 `*`
#   matches across the hierarchy delimiter (mbsync(1): "* matches anything and
#   % matches anything up to the next hierarchy delimiter"), so HEY/Screener is
#   already in scope. Worth stating, because the natural instinct on adding a
#   folder tree is to add a pattern for it, and an extra line here is an extra
#   chance to break the section with a stray blank one.
# Expunge Both is what makes a MOVE work at all. mbsync has no cross-folder
#   MOVE (isync 1.5.1); a local move is an append to the destination plus a
#   disappearance from the source, and this line is what turns that
#   disappearance into an actual expunge on iCloud rather than a message that
#   sits in INBOX flagged deleted — i.e. still in the phone's inbox, which is
#   the one thing phone parity exists to prevent.
# Patterns excludes "Notes": that folder is Apple Notes, which iCloud happens
# to store over IMAP. It is not email, notmuch would index every note as a
# message, and they would land in the Screener as mail from yourself. Verified
# present on this account with `mbsync -l icloud` (2026-08-17), alongside
# INBOX, Archive, Deleted Messages, Drafts, Junk and Sent Messages.
Channel icloud
Far :icloud-remote:
Near :icloud-local:
Patterns * !Notes
Create Both
Expunge Both
Remove None
SyncState *

Group icloud-all
Channel icloud
{{ end -}}
